    },
    {
      "claim": "The query's low neutral fraction (0.02) remains a physicochemical prior; no L4 record measures the query or its close alflutinib analog, so there is no affirmative F below 20% evidence.",
      "card_ids": ["C139", "C154"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "neutral_or_unclear",
      "decision_effect": "no_change",
      "card_ids": ["C139", "C154", "C169", "C174"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific or alflutinib-analog solubility or permeability measurement; L4 records are for nilotinib and other reference molecules.",
    "Nilotinib F of 31% is an assumed value and its solubility data are formulation- and media-dependent.",
    "The query is more ionized at physiological pH (neutral fraction 0.02) than nilotinib, so permeability transfer is uncertain.",
    "Food effects, dose, and formulation differences between the query and the gold contexts are unmeasured."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because the most transferable gold contexts (nilotinib 31% fasted, GSK1322322 57-77%) and L3 oral exposure for the close alflutinib analog support meaningful absorption; L4 adds a high human effective permeability estimate for nilotinib and shows solubility-limited but not threshold-defeating absorption for this chemotype. Low-F contexts are structurally distant, and the low physicochemical prior is not affirmative F < 20% evidence."
}

